<h2 style="color: #1a5276; text-align: center;">Lesson Planner – Theme # ___ – Lesson # 2</h2>
<p style="text-align: center; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">Complete this planner five times per theme, once per lesson (Lesson 1 through Lesson 5).</p>

<table style="border: 1px dashed #333; border-collapse: collapse; width: 100%; margin: 10px 0;">
    <tr style="border: 1px dashed #333;">
        <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;"><b>Lesson #:</b> 2</td>
        <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;"><b>Skills Focus:</b> Reading</td>
    </tr>
    <tr style="border: 1px dashed #333;">
        <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;"><b>Grade:</b> 3rd Grade (CEFR: A1.1)</td>
        <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;"><b>Scenario:</b> It's Time to Eat.</td>
        <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;"><b>Theme:</b> I Like Sancocho!</td>
    </tr>
    <tr style="border: 1px dashed #333;">
        <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;"><b>Date(s):</b> From ___ to ___</td>
        <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;" colspan="2"><b>Learning Sequence Time:</b> 45–50 min</td>
    </tr>
    <tr style="border: 1px dashed #333;">
        <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;" colspan="3">
            <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Specific Objective:</b> Reading: Show comprehension of texts by answering questions, both written and oral. Reading: Write responses to questions about a text. Phonemic Awareness: Blend spoken phonemes to form one-syllable or two-syllable words if these words have been previously encountered.</p>
            <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Learning Outcome:</b> Reading: Can show understanding of food preference texts by writing short responses (e.g., "What is his favorite food?" "He likes apples."). Can answer questions in writing about food choices based on short texts. (e.g., "What is his favorite food?" "He likes Sancocho."). Can combine individual phonemes to construct familiar one-syllable or two-syllable words, using prior knowledge of the words (e.g., blending /ˈæ-p-əl/ to form the word "apple").</p>
        </td>
    </tr>
</table>

<h3 style="color: #1a5276;">The Six Action-Oriented Approach (AOA) Lesson Stages</h3>
<table style="border: 1px dashed #333; border-collapse: collapse; width: 100%; margin: 10px 0;">
    <thead>
        <tr style="border: 1px dashed #333;">
            <th style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px; width: 33%;">Stage & Description</th>
            <th style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px; width: 33%;">Activities & Teacher Actions</th>
            <th style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px; width: 34%;">Estimated Date and Time</th>
        </tr>
    </thead>
    <tbody>
        <tr style="border: 1px dashed #333;">
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>STAGE 1 — Warm-up / Pre-task (Engagement, Modeling and Clarification)</b></p>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Warm-up:</b> The teacher starts by showing flashcards of familiar foods (apple, banana, sandwich, sancocho, juice, water). The teacher asks students, "What food is this?" and "Do you like [food]?" Students respond chorally or individually. The teacher introduces the concept of "favorite food" and prompts students to share theirs verbally.</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Modeling:</b> The teacher models sentences: "I like sancocho." and "I don't like bananas, but I like apples." The teacher then says, "My favorite food is sancocho." The teacher pronounces /f/ in "favorite" and /s/ in "snack" clearly, asking students to repeat. The teacher points to a simple written sentence on the board "I like ____." for students to read and complete orally, connecting to the reading focus.</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Clarification (CCQs):</b></p>
                <ul style="list-style-type: disc; margin-left: 20px;">
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">"If I say, 'I like sancocho,' do I want to eat it or not eat it?" (Expected answer: "Eat it!")</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">"If sancocho is my 'favorite food,' do I like it a little or a lot?" (Expected answer: "A lot!")</li>
                </ul>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">5-7 min</p>
            </td>
        </tr>
        <tr style="border: 1px dashed #333;">
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>STAGE 2 — Presentation</b></p>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Input of new language/content connected to Scenario "It's Time to Eat.":</b> The teacher presents a short, illustrated text on a projector or large printout titled "Our Favorite Meals." The text features simple descriptions from three different children about their favorite foods and mealtimes (breakfast, lunch, dinner, snack). It uses target vocabulary like "sancocho," "apple," "sandwich," "juice," "water," "healthy," "tasty," "eat," "drink," "favorite," and grammatical structures like "I like...", "He likes...", "She likes...", "What's your favorite food?", "Do you like...?", and "I like... but I don't like..."</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Material:</b> A short narrative text (3-4 sentences per child) with corresponding pictures of children and their foods (e.g., a short notice, illustrated dialogue). The register is informal and friendly, suitable for 3rd graders.</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Communicative Competence:</b> Understanding simple written descriptions of food preferences and mealtimes. Locating specific information within a short text.</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Comprehension tasks (Reading):</b></p>
                <ul style="list-style-type: disc; margin-left: 20px;">
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>Gist Question:</b> The teacher reads the title "Our Favorite Meals" and asks, "What is this text about?" (Expected answer: "Food" or "What people like to eat.")</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>First Reading:</b> The teacher reads the text aloud once, pointing to words. Students listen and follow along.</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>Second Reading:</b> Students read the text silently.</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>True/False Questions (Oral):</b> The teacher reads simple sentences based on the text (e.g., "Maria likes sancocho. True or False?"). Students point up for True, down for False.</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>Phonemic Awareness:</b> The teacher points to the words "apple" and "water" in the text. The teacher pronounces the phonemes: /ˈæ-p-əl/ and students blend to say "apple." The teacher says /ˈw-ɔːt-ər/ and students blend to say "water." The teacher also guides students to identify the /f/ sound in "favorite" and /s/ sound in "snack" from the text.</li>
                </ul>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">10-12 min</p>
            </td>
        </tr>
        <tr style="border: 1px dashed #333;">
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>STAGE 3 — Preparation / Practice</b></p>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Accuracy focus on Reading:</b> Activities will reinforce comprehension of food preference texts and writing short responses.</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Communicative Skill & Content:</b> Reading texts with WH- and yes/no questions, present simple (likes/dislikes), compound sentences with "but." Vocabulary: apple, banana, sandwich, lunch, breakfast, dinner, food, snack, water, juice, rice with chicken, soup, eat, drink, taste, favorite, healthy, tasty.</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Detailed Procedure:</b></p>
                <ul style="list-style-type: disc; margin-left: 20px;">
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>1. Controlled Practice (Read and Answer):</b> The teacher distributes a worksheet with the "Our Favorite Meals" text (or a slightly modified version) and 3-4 simple comprehension questions. Example questions: "What is Maria's favorite food?" (Students read the text and write "She likes sancocho."), "Does Pedro like juice?" (Students read and write "Yes, he does."). Students work individually to read the text and write their answers.</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>2. Semi-controlled Practice (Read and Match):</b> Students receive another worksheet. On one side, there are short sentences describing food preferences (e.g., "I like healthy food. My favorite is apple and water."), and on the other side, there are pictures of the foods. Students read each sentence and draw a line to match it to the correct picture. This activity also includes sentences with "but" (e.g., "I like bananas, but I don't like soup.").</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>3. Pair Check (Read and Discuss):</b> In pairs, students exchange their completed worksheets from activity 1. They take turns reading their answers to their partner and checking if the answers match what is in the text. The teacher circulates, provides support, and monitors for correct reading and comprehension. Example exchange: Student A reads: "What is Maria's favorite food?" Student B reads their written answer: "She likes sancocho."</li>
                </ul>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">15-18 min</p>
            </td>
        </tr>
        <tr style="border: 1px dashed #333;">
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>STAGE 4 — Performance / Production</b></p>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Communicative task:</b> Students will read short profiles of new characters and write short responses to questions about their food preferences and meal choices, integrating grammar and vocabulary in a realistic scenario related to "It's Time to Eat."</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Task format:</b> Individual written response based on reading provided texts.</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Detailed Procedure:</b></p>
                <ul style="list-style-type: disc; margin-left: 20px;">
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">The teacher gives each student a worksheet with three short, new character profiles. Each profile includes a name, a picture, and 2-3 simple sentences about their food preferences (e.g., "This is Mateo. He likes rice with chicken for lunch. He doesn't like bananas, but he likes apples. His favorite drink is juice. He thinks sancocho is tasty!").</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Below each profile, there are 2-3 WH- questions and a yes/no question for students to answer in writing (e.g., "What is Mateo's favorite drink?", "What does he like for lunch?", "Does he like bananas?").</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Students read each profile carefully and write their responses using complete sentences where appropriate (e.g., "His favorite drink is juice.", "He likes rice with chicken for lunch.", "No, he doesn't.").</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">The teacher encourages students to refer directly to the text for their answers, focusing on accurate comprehension and written expression.</li>
                </ul>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">10-12 min</p>
            </td>
        </tr>
        <tr style="border: 1px dashed #333;">
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>STAGE 5 — Assessment / Post-task</b></p>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Formative assessment strategy:</b> Teacher observation during the reading and writing tasks, and collection of student written responses for review.</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Assessment Task:</b> Students read a short, new paragraph (3-4 sentences) about a person describing their favorite food for dinner. Then, students answer two WH- questions and one yes/no question about the text in writing on an exit slip or their worksheet.</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Evaluation Criteria:</b></p>
                <ul style="list-style-type: disc; margin-left: 20px;">
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Accuracy of answers (shows comprehension of the text).</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Use of target vocabulary (e.g., food names, 'like', 'favorite').</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Clarity and legibility of written responses.</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Ability to form simple sentences for answers.</li>
                </ul>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Student Self-Assessment / Exit Ticket:</b> Students complete two sentences on their exit slip:</p>
                <ul style="list-style-type: disc; margin-left: 20px;">
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">"Today I read about _________________________ (food/people)."</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">"I can write answers about _________________________ (favorite food/what people like)."</li>
                </ul>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">5 min</p>
            </td>
        </tr>
        <tr style="border: 1px dashed #333;">
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>STAGE 6 — Reflection</b></p>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Student self-reflection prompt:</b></p>
                <ul style="list-style-type: disc; margin-left: 20px;">
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">"What new words did I read today about food?"</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">"Was it easy or difficult to answer the questions by reading the text?"</li>
                </ul>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Teacher reflection:</b></p>
                <ul style="list-style-type: disc; margin-left: 20px;">
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">What worked well in this lesson? (e.g., student engagement with the texts, ability to identify specific information).</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">What challenges did students face in reading or writing responses? What adjustments or additional scaffolds might be needed for future reading lessons?</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">How can the phonemic awareness activity be integrated more effectively?</li>
                </ul>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Connection to next lesson:</b> Today's reading practice of understanding food preferences and simple questions will directly prepare students for Lesson 3 (Speaking & Interaction), where they will verbally ask and answer questions about food preferences with their peers.</p>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">3-5 min</p>
            </td>
        </tr>
    </tbody>
</table>